{{Politics of Balisca}}

The '''Open Cabildo''' ({{Langx|bl|Cabildo abierto}}; {{Langx|mxi|كَابِيلْدُو أَ بْيِيرْتُو|Kabildu abyírtu}}), is the Baliscan {{W|open source governance|open-source}} {{W|civic participation|online civic platform}} that is {{W|Platform cooperative|democratically and cooperatively}} run. It forms the backbone of Baliscan {{W|direct democracy|direct}} {{W|e-democracy}} designed to give citizens the power to engage in {{W|participatory democracy|participatory}} decision-making on a variety of issues. The Open Cabildo allows citizens to create and manage their own assemblies, discussions, and voting processes. Since 2022, the platform has become increasingly influential to the incumbent [[Bilal II Council]], which has lauded the project as an innovative and effective approach to democratic governance in Balisca's [[W:Information society|information society]].

== History ==

=== Development and structure ===
The Open Cabildo was first conceptualized in the early 2010s by a coalition of digital rights activists, educators, and cooperative networks across the confederation. Initially developed as a localized decision-making platform for urban planning and participatory budgeting, it gained traction during the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]], when digital forums were needed for rapid consensus building. The platform was officially endorsed by the [[Confederal Congress]] in 2022 following widespread grassroots adoption and became integrated into confederal administration as part of the Bilal II administration's civic transparency reforms

The Open Cabildo is built on a fork of the open-source platform [[W:Decidim|Decidim]], with heavy customization to meet confederal privacy standards and federated infrastructure. It supports [[W:Peer-to-peer communication|peer-to-peer communication]], [[W:Local caching|local caching]] for offline access, and robust [[W:Encryption protocols|encryption protocols]] for identity protection. Each commune maintains a local node that federates with other regions using an [[W:InterPlanetary File System|IPFS-based]] ledger and time stamping protocol. This enables decentralized governance without centralized data warehousing, consistent with the principles of the [[Carta Social|''Carta Social'']].

== Key concepts ==
{{Main|Direct democracy|Participatory democracy}}
'''Creating and managing assemblies''': Once registered and having confirmed their eligibility, citizens can create and manage their own assemblies, which can either be open to the public or restricted to specific groups of people at a time. Assemblies are often organized around single/specific issues, as small as local issues to national issues such as {{W|environmental policy}}, {{W|healthcare}}, or {{W|education}}. The members of the assembly can participate in discussions, propose ideas, and vote on decisions. These are often called 'wiki bills' due to their {{W|collaborative governance|collaborative}} nature.

'''Participatory decision-making''': Open Cabildo provides several tools for participatory decision-making, such as polls, surveys, and debates. All citizens can propose ideas and submit them for consideration by the assembly. Members of the assembly can then discuss the idea, suggest modifications, and vote on whether or not to adopt it. This is done through a [[W:Liquid democracy|blended "liquid" democracy]] system, which means that citizens can either vote directly on the policy or delegate their vote to elected officials in the [[Confederal Congress]].

'''Coordinated decision-making''': The Open Cabildo platform also provides tools for coordinated decision-making, such as {{W|consensus democracy|consensus-building and collective decision-making}}. The platform also includes tools for collective intelligence and enabling assemblies to collaboratively refine proposals. Mechanisms like iterative polling, consent-based decision-making, and negotiation spaces allow users to co-develop policies acceptable to a broad majority.

'''Monitoring and accountability''': Open Cabildo allows citizens to monitor the decision-making process and hold their elected representatives in the [[Confederal Congress]] accountable for their actions. Elected delegates in the [[Confederal Congress]] are also required to maintain public profiles where their participation, endorsements, and deliberative history are fully accessible. This infrastructure fosters a culture of [[W:government transparency|transparency]], accountability, and reciprocal civic oversight. Users can also view the records of all discussions, proposals, and votes, and can track the progress of decisions from proposal to implementation. This transparency helps to build trust between citizens and their elected officials, fostering a sense of cooperation and shared responsibility.

== Access and security ==
{{Further|Communal key}}

=== Language support ===
The Open Cabildo is available in the confederal [[W:Working languages|working language]] ([[Baliscano]]), the seven territorialized languages ([[Aragonese language|Aragonese]], [[W:Catalan language|Catalan]], [[W:Judaeo-Spanish|Judaeo-Spanish]] (Ladino), [[Occitan language|Occitan]], [[Mozarabic]], [[Kurupirangüe language|Kurupiran]] and [[W:Mapudungun language|Mapudungun]]), along with languages spoken by immigrant communities, like [[W:English language|English]], [[W:Varieties of Chinese|Chinese]] ([[W:Mandarin|Mandarin]] and [[W:Cantonese|Cantonese]]), [[W:Hachijo dialect|Hachijo]], [[w:Portuguese language|Portuguese]], [[w:Spanish language|Spanish]], and [[Sugihan language|Sugihan]]. Additionally, it features a screen reader-friendly design for users with visual impairments.

=== Privacy ===

# '''Verified proof-of-personhood, authenticated through federated digital identities'''; This refers to the system’s ability to confirm that every participant in the Open Cabildo is a real, unique individual without requiring their personal data to be centralized or exposed. The [[communal key]] functions as a [[W:Federated identity|federated]] [[W:Digital identity|digital ID]] — when someone participates in public debate or votes, their identity is [[W:Key (cryptography)|cryptographically]] verified using a decentralized protocol tied to their communal registry and syndical networks. This ensures no fake users, [[W:Internet bot|bots]], or duplicate accounts can engage in democratic processes, while preserving privacy and autonomy. 
# '''Federated time-stamping to guarantee authorship and chronological integrity'''; When a user submits a legislative proposal, comment, or vote, the system automatically applies a federated timestamp — a cryptographically verified mark that records exactly when and by whom (via their key) the action was taken. This allows for open audit trails and helps maintain the integrity of civic records without relying on centralized servers. Disputes over authorship, edits, or vote timing can be resolved through these timestamped logs.
# '''Optional pseudonymity, tied to verified key identities'''; To ensure freedom of expression while still maintaining civic accountability, users may choose [[W:Pseudonym|pseudonyms]] for public deliberation. However, these pseudonyms are anchored to a real key identity behind the scenes. This approach allows people to speak, organize, or dissent without fear of [[W:Government surveillance|surveillance]] or retaliation — yet still ensures that only verified residents are participating. This model deters manipulation, trolling, or automated disruption while supporting open and pluralistic values.

== Reactions ==

=== Domestic ===
Open Cabildo has become a symbolic institution within Baliscan civil society, especially among the youth, [[Cooperatives in Balisca|cooperatives]], and [[Autonomous commune|autonomous communes]]. It is commonly taught in schools as part of [[W:Digital citizenship in education|digital citizenship education]], and its structure has been proposed, adopted or adapted by other communities aligned with post-capitalist governance models. The platform is often cited in international forums as a rare example of digital infrastructure built on the principles of [[W:decentralization|decentralization]], equity, and consent-driven participation.

==== Criticism ====
While widely praised, the Open Cabildo has faced criticism, particularly from scholars and elders in rural communes have expressed concern that digital-first governance risks alienating older generations. Others cite occasional "delegate fatigue," where active participants must balance civic engagement with everyday obligations. In rare cases, pseudonymous accounts have been used for [[W:Trolling|trolling]] or [[W:Disinformation|disinformation]], though the use of federated proof-of-personhood protocols have largely mitigated abuse. 

